1	//! Grading. Today the run grade ([`RunGrade`]) — a measurement
2	//! run scored from its own time-ordered [`BatchSummary`] series.
3	//! The environment grade lands here too at 0.23.0-4, measured
4	//! from the warmup micro-probe; the two answer different
5	//! questions and print as separate letters.
6	//!
7	//! The calibration grade certified a ~1 s window *before* the run
8	//! — the room, not the exam. The run grade scores the exam: every
9	//! signal is computed from the batches the run itself produced,
10	//! so the letter describes the data being reported.
11	//!
12	//! - Four signals, each scored against its own ascending cutoffs
13	//!   (A..F); the composite letter is the worst of them, and each
14	//!   signal prints its own letter beside its value so the
15	//!   composite always names its cause. See
16	//!   [`RunGrade::scores`] for the arithmetic and
17	//!   [`thresholds`] for the cutoffs.
18	//! - **Four, where the calibration grade has six.** The mapping,
19	//!   and why two have no run-side analog:
20	//!   - `disturbed` → `interference` — the census rate, same
21	//!     definition rebased on the batch's own floor.
22	//!   - `dirty windows` → `bursts` — window becomes batch.
23	//!   - `drift` → `drift` — floor movement across the
24	//!     measurement, unchanged in spirit.
25	//!   - `repeat` → `step` — `repeat` compares constants between
26	//!     two clean calibration attempts, a transition detector at
27	//!     attempt-to-attempt scale where `drift` is the same
28	//!     detector within one window. A single run has no second
29	//!     attempt, so the equivalent question inside one run is
30	//!     whether the floor shifted partway through: the split
31	//!     detector.
32	//!   - `resid` and `cross` — **no analog, deliberately.** Both
33	//!     grade the quality of a *fit*: the worst residual of a
34	//!     ladder point against the Theil-Sen line, and the
35	//!     loop-only slope against the dithered two-point fit. They
36	//!     exist because calibration fits a line through a multi-N
37	//!     ladder. A bench run fits nothing — no line for a point
38	//!     to sit off, no second estimator to cross-check — so
39	//!     run-side versions would mean inventing the fit first.
40	//! - Two of the four watch the run's floor move under the
41	//!   measurement: `drift` asks whether the run ended where it
42	//!   began, `step` looks for a shift anywhere inside it and says
43	//!   when. A run that shifts and shifts back is invisible to the
44	//!   first and obvious to the second.
45	//! - The other two split contamination by how it is distributed:
46	//!   `interference` is the run-wide census rate (how many samples
47	//!   sat above their batch's floor), `bursts` the fraction of
48	//!   batches whose mean sits above the typical one (whether that
49	//!   interference was localized in time or spread evenly).
50	//!
51	//! The run grade **reports; it does not warn**. Its signals are facts
52	//! about the run, and a run's character is largely the workload's:
53	//! on a quiet 7600x the blocking mpsc round-trips read step F
54	//! whether pinned or not, while the same round-trip spinning reads
55	//! A — we think that is park/unpark bimodality, which belongs to
56	//! the bench, not the box. An F there is a true description of
57	//! what was measured, not a fault to raise, and the report's job
58	//! is a faithful histogram rather than a verdict. The verdict on
59	//! the box is a separate grade measured during warmup, where the
60	//! workload's character hasn't entered the numbers yet.
61	
62	use crate::harness::BatchSummary;
63	
64	/// Grade thresholds, one array per signal: the ascending cutoffs
65	/// a signal crosses to score B, C, D, F (below the first is A).
66	///
67	/// - **Provisional** — seeded from the 3900X's quiet-run spread
68	///   on 2026-07-27 and checked against a quiet 7600x on
69	///   2026-07-28, where ten back-to-back `min-now` runs all read A
70	///   and 12 of 16 benches in one sweep read A.
71	/// - Calibrated on a fast single-threaded bench, where the run's
72	///   letter is mostly the box. A blocking multi-threaded bench
73	///   carries OS involvement in its own numbers and reads worse by
74	///   nature — correctly, since it *is* less steady. Nothing here
75	///   raises a warning, so a low letter costs a reader nothing
76	///   beyond the fact itself.
77	pub mod thresholds {
78	    /// Run-wide census rate: samples above their batch's
79	    /// over-floor cut, as a fraction of all samples.
80	    pub const INTERFERENCE: [f64; 4] = [0.02, 0.05, 0.12, 0.30];
81	    /// Fraction of batches whose mean sits [`BURST_TOL`] above the
82	    /// run's median batch mean.
83	    pub const BURSTS: [f64; 4] = [0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.90];
84	    /// Floor movement from the run's first quarter to its last.
85	    pub const DRIFT: [f64; 4] = [0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.10];
86	    /// Largest floor shift across any split of the run.
87	    pub const STEP: [f64; 4] = [0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.10];
88	}
89	
90	/// A batch is "hot" when its mean exceeds the run's *median*
91	/// batch mean by this fraction — it carried something the typical
92	/// batch did not. The fraction of hot batches says whether the
93	/// contamination was localized in time or spread over the run.
94	///
95	/// - The reference is the median, not the quietest batch: the
96	///   minimum is an extreme, and against it a bench with any real
97	///   spread reads ~100% hot (mpsc-2t read 98% on a run whose
98	///   batch means varied normally).
99	pub const BURST_TOL: f64 = 0.05;
100	
101	/// Minimum batches on each side of a candidate split point for
102	/// the `step` detector to consider it. Below this a "transition"
103	/// is one or two batches — a burst, which the `bursts` signal
104	/// already counts.
105	pub const MIN_SPLIT_BATCHES: usize = 4;
106	
107	/// Score one signal against its [`thresholds`] array: 0 (A)
108	/// through 4 (F) — the count of cutoffs crossed.
109	fn score(x: f64, cutoffs: [f64; 4]) -> u8 {
110	    cutoffs.iter().filter(|&&c| x > c).count() as u8
111	}
112	
113	/// Map a 0..=4 score to its letter (no 'E': 4 is 'F').
114	fn score_letter(s: u8) -> char {
115	    match s {
116	        0 => 'A',
117	        1 => 'B',
118	        2 => 'C',
119	        3 => 'D',
120	        _ => 'F',
121	    }
122	}
